#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167814} :activity/id #uuid "17ec46bf-0215-5718-96b0-bb3a2bf59e99" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049852 :topic/id #uuid "18c7c8d4-f8df-5ab7-94e4-a5bda9fa7a9b" :topic/math-academy-id 3238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167979 :tutorial/content "A **decoupled system** of ODEs is a system in which each equation involves only its own dependent variable. For a linear homogeneous system, this means each equation has the form\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nFor example, the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis decoupled, while the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled.\n\nIn matrix form, a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nis decoupled exactly when $A$ is a diagonal matrix. For example:\n\n- The system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- The system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal." :tutorial/id #uuid "aa68d83f-b5d2-5a65-94aa-55bcc6662e27" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14185 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ceac62c9-e3b9-5362-9713-cd359b4c8bd9" :step/math-academy-id 577532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166505 :question/id #uuid "a19f597d-f584-57b3-9e67-cb4cea07c876" :question/math-academy-id "e-21120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048451}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365603}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "9469c82f-9106-5aaa-9ac6-c68a6af9fdf8" :step/math-academy-id 577533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167816}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167816 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167980 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider the following decoupled system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is diagonal, we can solve the two differential equations separately using elementary calculus:\n\n- The solution to $x_1'(t)={3}x_1(t)$ is $x_1(t)=c_1e^{{3}t}$ for $c_1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- The solution to $x_2'(t)={-5}x_2(t)$ is $x_2(t)=c_2e^{{-5}t}$ for $c_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the solution of the system as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn general, for a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe formula for the **general solution** is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the set \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nalways forms a linearly independent set of fundamental solutions to the system." :tutorial/id #uuid "77332312-6432-5686-83b4-0d3679918910" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14186 :tutorial/title "Solving a Decoupled System: General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "a5af5357-82ea-53cb-a12f-38de66e5fda8" :step/math-academy-id 577536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167817}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166506 :question/id #uuid "83ebd59d-883f-510a-9bc9-035b3088a43c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21123"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce35e5a9-2444-5e5f-9463-5a4d54496d9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050221}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366344}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Decoupled Linear System of Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "a2da3849-4b6a-5fd0-8a4a-234a559d5212" :step/math-academy-id 577537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167818}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167981 :tutorial/content "Consider the following decoupled system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the *general solution* to this is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBut sometimes we may need to find a **particular solution** where we give specific values to the constants $c_1$ and $c_2.$\n\nFor example, suppose that $x_1$ and $x_2$ represent the number of birds of species $A$ and $B,$ respectively, that inhabit a particular island, and the above system models the growth rates of the two species. \n\nGiven this information, we might want to know how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years. To answer this question, we need an **initial condition**, which is some extra information about the system at a particular moment in time. \n\nSuppose that at $t=0,$ there were initially $20$ birds of species $A$ and $2\\,000$ birds of species $B.$ We can write this initial condition as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(0) \\\\ {x}_{2}(0)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, by substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we can find specific values of $c_1$ and $c_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $c_1 = 20$ and $c_2 = 2\\,000$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years by substituting $t=3$ into the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =20[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3}+2\\,000[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\approx [\\begin{aligned}401.7 \\\\ 99.6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that there will be approximately $402$ birds of species $A$ and $100$ birds of species $B$ after $t=3$ years." :tutorial/id #uuid "7c726646-cb34-5660-97ad-19e831208913" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14187 :tutorial/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Particular Solution"} :step/id #uuid "11b39563-b10a-5e01-ac10-df79617b448a" :step/math-academy-id 577540 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166507 :question/id #uuid "f5136df2-fcf6-5f64-aeda-f14a91231788" :question/math-academy-id "e-21124"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392285}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "a30dd2b1-d654-5565-a8d1-66a0df7983dc" :step/math-academy-id 577541}] :activity/title "Solving Decoupled Homogeneous Systems of ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167009}]}
